=> Multiway Search Trees
    -> Multiway Tree
        -> A tree in which a node can have more than two children.
        -> Unlike a Binary Tree, it is not limited to two children.

    -> Multiway Search Tree
        -> A Multiway Tree that stores multiple sorted keys within a node.
        -> Keys divide data into different search ranges.
        -> Child pointers help navigate to the correct subtree.

    -> Multiple Keys Within One Node
        -> One node can store multiple keys in sorted order.
        -> Example: [20 | 40 | 60] represents one node containing 3 keys.
        -> Keys are search values, whereas nodes are containers holding keys.

    -> Multiple Child Pointers
        -> A child pointer refers to another node.
        -> An internal node with K keys can have up to K + 1 children.
        -> Example: 3 keys can divide searching into 4 ranges.
        -> Leaf nodes do not have children.

    -> Search Example
                         [20 | 40 | 60]
                        /     |    |     \
                       P1    P2   P3     P4
        -> Root Node: [20 | 40 | 60]
        -> P1: Values less than 20.
        -> P2: Values between 20 and 40.
        -> P3: Values between 40 and 60.
        -> P4: Values greater than 60.
        -> Searching for 35 means following P2.

    -> Node Capacity
        -> Maximum number of keys allowed inside a node.
        -> Example: Capacity = 3, Node = [10 | 20 | 30].
        -> Inserting 40 into this full node would cause overflow.
        -> B-Trees and B+ Trees handle overflow using structural adjustments such as splitting.

    -> Fan-Out
        -> Fan-out means the number of children connected to a node.
        -> Actual fan-out = Number of existing children.
        -> Maximum fan-out = Maximum number of children allowed.
        -> A node with maximum key capacity K can have up to K + 1 children.

        -> Example: Maximum Key Capacity = 3
            -> Consider a parent node containing 3 keys: [20 | 40 | 60].
            -> Maximum possible child pointers = 3 + 1 = 4.
            -> We will gradually increase the number of children.

        -> Case 1: Only 1 Child (Actual Fan-Out = 1)

                        [20 | 40 | 60]
                        |
                        [10]

            -> Only one child exists.
            -> Actual fan-out = 1.
            -> Maximum possible fan-out = 4.
            -> The other 3 child positions are empty.

        -> Case 2: Two Children (Actual Fan-Out = 2)

                        [20 | 40 | 60]
                        /           \
                    [10]          [70]

            -> Two children exist.
            -> Actual fan-out = 2.
            -> Maximum possible fan-out = 4.
            -> Two child positions are empty.

        -> Case 3: Three Children (Actual Fan-Out = 3)

                        [20 | 40 | 60]
                    /     |       \
                    [10]   [30]     [70]

            -> Three children exist.
            -> Actual fan-out = 3.
            -> Maximum possible fan-out = 4.
            -> One child position is empty.

        -> Case 4: Four Children (Actual Fan-Out = 4)

                        [20 | 40 | 60]
                    /     |    |    \
                    [10]  [30] [50]  [70]

            -> All four child positions are occupied.
            -> Actual fan-out = 4.
            -> Maximum possible fan-out = 4.
            -> This node has reached its maximum fan-out.

        -> Key Observation
            -> The parent node contains 3 keys in every case.
            -> Its maximum fan-out remains 4.
            -> Its actual fan-out increases from 1 to 2 to 3 to 4.
            -> Having more keys does not automatically mean all child positions are occupied.
            -> These examples represent a general Multiway Search Tree, not a standard B-Tree or B+ Tree internal node.

        -> Importance of Fan-Out
            -> Higher fan-out allows a node to connect to more subtrees.
            -> More child branches help divide searching into more ranges.
            -> Higher fan-out generally reduces tree height when nodes are well populated.
            -> A shorter tree can reduce database page reads during searching.

    -> Tree Height
        -> Height is the longest downward path from the root to a leaf, measured in edges.
        -> Higher fan-out generally reduces tree height when nodes are well populated.
        -> A smaller height means fewer node levels need to be visited.
        -> This can reduce database page reads during searching.

    -> Binary Search Tree vs Multiway Search Tree
        -> BST: Usually one key per node and maximum 2 children.
        -> Multiway Search Tree: Multiple keys per node and potentially more than 2 children.
        -> BST uses one key to divide searching into two ranges.
        -> Multiway Search Tree uses multiple keys to divide searching into several ranges.

    -> Practical Java and MySQL Connection
        -> Java object references help illustrate child pointers conceptually.
        -> MySQL InnoDB uses B+ Tree indexes based on multiway searching.
        -> Internal index pages contain search information and child page references.
        -> Large fan-out helps databases search records using fewer tree levels.